Q) So here we need to find the minimum amount or energy required to reach the top of the stairs, but i have encountere two types of questions here.
in one there is an amount provided for each stairs and we have to keep adding that to our total amount spent (this is incase of min. amount 
required to reach the top), here we can jump either i+1 step or i+2 steps where i is the step we are in.
// i feel here we need to be taking the min of i-1 and i-2 and keep adding till the end, base case we can keep for steps 0 and 1;
like for prev 2 = 0 and prev 1 = stairs[0], and we keep loop from (int i=2;i<n;i++); 
we will take left and right as variables and keep left as stairs[i] + prev1;
and right as stairs[i] + prev2; and then take curr = min(left,right); 
also the function to take mod is abs(), its not needed here but in the frog jump question there was a little change and we need this
abs() func. 
I feel the T.C will be O(n) since the no. of operations (in for loop) is equal to the imput size and S.C will be O(1) 
since no matter how large the input size becomes no extra memory is required.

int mincost(int n){
int prev1 = stairs[0];
int prev2 = 0;
int curr;
int onestep;
int twostep;
for(int i = 1; i<n; i++){
onestep = stairs[i] + prev1;
if(i>1){twostep = stairs[i] + prev2;} // this is important since if you are in the first step you cant go two steps back so you can choose prev2 only when you are from step 2 and forward.
else{twostep = stairs[i];}
curr = min(onestep,twostep);
prev2 = prev1;
prev1 = curr;
}
return min(curr, prev2);   // this is done since you can reach the last step from n-1 or n-2 steps thats why you need to check the min of 
the last two steps;
}
